      "text": "1. **Calculate the slant height of the pyramid:**\n   The slant height \\( l \\) of the pyramid can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid, the distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of a side, and the slant height itself. The distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of a side is half the diagonal of the square base.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Diagonal of the base} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance from center to midpoint} = \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   l = \\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{2})^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{50 + 144} = \\sqrt{194} \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the total area of the four triangular faces:**\n   Each triangular face has a base of 10 cm and a slant height of \\(\\sqrt{194}\\) cm. The area of one triangular face is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times \\sqrt{194} = 5\\sqrt{194} \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n   Since there are four such triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area of the four triangular faces} = 4 \\times 5\\sqrt{194} = 20\\sqrt{194} \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the area of the square base:**\n   The area of the square base is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the square base} = 10^2 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total surface area:**\n   The total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the square base and the total area of the four triangular faces:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total surface area} = 100 + 20\\sqrt{194} \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{100 + 20\\sqrt{194}}\\) square centimeters.",
